{"chunk_id": "fea1f0c68291", "text": "This section of the Bhagavad Gita (Chapter IV) presents Krishna explaining the ancient origin and spiritual purpose of his teaching on devotion and action. He describes how the doctrine was passed through royal sages but was lost over time, prompting him to reveal it again to Arjuna. Krishna clarifies his divine nature: though unborn and eternal, he incarnates whenever righteousness declines and injustice rises, protecting the virtuous and restoring moral order. The chapter explores the paradox of action\u2014true wisdom lies in acting without attachment to results. Various forms of sacrifice are described, culminating in the claim that the sacrifice of knowledge is highest because knowledge burns away the bondage of action. Faith, disciplined senses, and guidance from the wise lead to liberating knowledge that destroys doubt and leads to spiritual peace.\nKrishna explains his divine incarnations, the nature of action and inaction, and the supremacy of knowledge and selfless sacrifice for liberation.\nThemes: divine incarnation, karma and inaction, selfless action, sacrifice, spiritual knowledge", "vector": [0.005734506528824568, -0.015704026445746422, -0.01396057941019535, 0.004800982307642698, -0.018579410389065742, 0.004026839975267649, -0.025787388905882835, -0.023484479635953903, -0.022703832015395164, -0.005282381549477577, 0.020934363827109337, 0.010356590151786804, -0.002039441606029868, 0.010851000435650349, -0.004921332001686096, -0.01252939272671938, 0.04728121683001518, -0.012405790388584137, 0.0223265178501606, 0.00968002900481224, -0.009276694618165493, -0.009725566953420639, -0.013205953873693943, -0.005201064050197601, -0.00587437255308032, 0.005763780791312456, 0.008092712610960007, -0.029560519382357597, 0.009868685156106949, -0.030861597508192062, -0.01550886407494545, -0.00473918067291379, -0.009881696663796902, -0.00717545161023736, -0.029742669314146042, -0.0014897356741130352, 0.00559789314866066, -0.02979